n and the variance of  these protests has prompted India's  Prime Minister Narendra Modi to respond  on Twitter he called the outcry  unfortunate and deeply distressed  distressing he assured people that no  Indian has anything to worry regarding  this act he also added that this is the  time to maintain peace unity and  brotherhood and joining me now a South  Asia Director of Human Rights Watch  Meenakshi Ganguly Miss goggly Prime  Minister Modi has appealed for calm in  the midst of these violent protests do  you think the extent of the opposition  has caught the government by surprise I  do hope that that is the case the Prime  Minister now recognizes that there is  public opinion against this and that he  does the right thing in in in reversing  these policies you know democracy it's  very important for her for a for an  elected leader the heat protests instead  of threatening protesters or making this  about politics but do you think that  these protests that we are seeing across  the country represent the large majority  view on the citizenship Amendment Act it  is hard to tell that the question really  is that there has been a very  significant number of voices students  who were out on the streets they the  government has made different remarks on  this issue  initially it was all about you know  trying to control immigration which of  course is a is also a problematic  because it is immigration trying to  control immigration based on religion  but the bigger concern really is how  this amendment bill is tied to a  national citizenship process now what  happens in a citizenship verification  process is that if a non-muslim cannot  produce the paperwork they will  automatically be granted citizenship  while a Muslim would not be able to now  in a country where we know for a fact  that paperwork is extremely hard to  produce this this leads to concerns that  a significant chunk of India's  population may be denied citizenship and  that really is the basis for 